  "nameStory": "The salmon pinkgill gets its name from two key features that make it easy to remember - its warm orange cap that resembles the color of salmon flesh, and its distinctive pinkish-brown spore print that's characteristic of the pinkgill family. Like all pinkgills, when you take a spore print by placing the cap gill-side down on white paper overnight, you'll see those telltale pink-brown spores that give this whole group its common name.",
  "description": "The salmon pinkgill is a small to medium-sized mushroom with a bright orange cap that really catches your eye in the forest. It's quite petite, with caps reaching only about 1.5 inches across, but the stem can stretch up to 4 inches tall, giving it a somewhat elegant appearance. This colorful little fungus belongs to the pinkgill family, a group known for their pink to brown spore prints. You'll often find them growing from the soil near coniferous trees, especially after periods of rain.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "The pinkish-brown spore print is the most reliable way to confirm you've found a pinkgill - place the cap gill-side down on white paper overnight to see this diagnostic feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Bright orange cap up to 1.5 inches wide with a smooth texture that feels slightly sticky when moist",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Slender stem reaching up to 4 inches tall, usually paler than the cap and lacking any ring or bulbous base",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Gills start pale but develop pinkish tones as spores mature, attached to the stem in a way that creates small notches",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Pleasant, mild mushroomy smell without any strong or unpleasant odors",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for salmon pinkgills in coniferous forests throughout New England, particularly under pine, spruce, and fir trees. They prefer moist, rich soil and often appear along forest paths or in clearings where the soil stays consistently damp. You'll find them growing directly from the ground rather than on rotting wood.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Early salmon pinkgills may appear in late spring after warm rains, especially in southern New England where the season starts earlier.",
    "summer": "Peak season for salmon pinkgills, with the most abundant fruiting occurring after summer thunderstorms provide the moisture they need.",
    "fall": "Fruiting continues into early fall, particularly during the wet September weather that's common in New England.",
    "winter": "No active fruiting - the mushrooms have released their spores and the mycelium rests underground until conditions improve."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being found on four different continents, the salmon pinkgill was first scientifically described right here in North America back in 1859, making it one of our 'native discoveries' that later turned out to have relatives around the world!",
  "confusionNotes": "The salmon pinkgill can look very similar to some waxy caps (Hygrocybe species), especially the witch's hat, which also has a bright orange cap. The key difference is the spore print - waxy caps have white spores while pinkgills have that distinctive pink-brown print. Remember: 'Pink print = pinkgill!'",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The pinkgill family (Entolomataceae) used to be much more confusing for beginners because many species were scattered across different genera, but modern classification has helped group most of the pink-spored mushrooms together, making identification more logical.",
  "compareHints": "Among its pinkgill relatives, salmon pinkgill stands out for its consistently bright orange color - the yellow unicorn entoloma is more yellow, while the aborted entoloma often appears as irregular white blobs. The wood pinkgill grows on decaying logs rather than soil, and most other pinkgills in our region tend toward brown, gray, or purple tones rather than this species' distinctive salmon-orange hue.",
